# ESP32-OTA-with-BootGuard
ESP32 OTA with BootGuard, a self-built safety net that detects crash loops and rolls back automatically.

| ![Setup](images/setup.jpg) | ![OLED](images/oled.jpg)

## The problem
A broken OTA update can leave a remote device unreachable. The update exists, but it cannot be delivered so someone has to physically go to the device with a USB cable.

## The discovery
1. I enabled the official rollback: the build flag CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE  plus the approval call in the code. The first test seemed to work.

2. After repeating the test, the broken firmware was in an endless loop. The first result was a false positive. The bootloader never intervened.

3. The cause: PlatformIO’s Arduino framework uses a precompiled bootloader. The build flag reaches only my own code, the bootloader is already built without rollback support, so the flag stays on, the code compiles and nothing happens

4. So I built my own mechanism: BootGuard.

## The solution (BootGuard)
- A boot counter is stored in NVS (a storage that survives restarts and crashes)

- On every boot: the counter increases by one, early in setup, before anything can crash.

- If setup reaches the end, the counter resets to 0 and the boot is considered successful.

- If the counter exceeds 3, the device is stuck in a crash loop.

- Then BootGuard intervenes: it resets the counter to 0 (preventing ping-pong between partitions), switches the boot partition to the previous version, and restarts.

## How I tested it
- First I tested the counter separately. Healthy resets stayed at one, forced crashes stacked up to the threshold.

- Then the full test: I pushed a deliberately broken firmware over OTA.

- Result: BootGuard detected the crash loop and rolled back automatically to the working version

## Requirements
This project requires: An ESP32 board and PlatformIO with the Arduino framework. It uses a custom partition table with two app partitions, because without the two partitions there is nothing to roll back to. Add your WiFi data to a separate file like secret.h (secret.h is just an example, you can name it how you want). 


## ✨ Features

- The BootGuard (self-built rollback mechanism)
- OTA updates
- Crash loop detection
- Automatic rollback
- WiFi timeout with automatic retry

## 🧰 Components Required

- ESP32 Development Board (e.g., ESP32 DevKit)
- SSD1306 OLED display (128x64, I²C)
- Breadboard and jumper wires
- Micro-USB cable 

---

## 🔌 Wiring (I²C Bus)

| ESP32 Pin | Function | Connected To |
|---------- |----------|--------------|
| 3.3V      | Power    | VCC of OLED |
| GND |       Ground   | GND of OLED |
| GPIO21    | SDA      | SDA of OLED |
| GPIO22    | SCL      | SCL of OLED |

## ▶️ Usage

- This project uses a custom partitions.csv with two app partitions
- First flash must be over USB; after that, updates can go over OTA

1. Install required libraries via PlatformIO — add these to `platformio.ini`:

```ini
lib_deps =
   adafruit/Adafruit SSD1306@^2.5.7
   adafruit/Adafruit GFX Library@^1.11.6
```

